7. THESIS-READY SUMMARY PARAGRAPH
====================================================================================================
  The A1 scaling experiment ramped from 1 to 20 concurrent Flink-path producers, adding one
  device every 3 minutes for ~60 min total.  Across all captured data (25380 messages,
  423 of 630 expected windows = 67% system coverage),
  median end-to-end latency was 39046 ms (p99 = 142090 ms,
  max = 240696 ms).
  At 1 active device the median latency was 33877 ms; at 19 active devices it was
  42256 ms (p99 = 78479 ms) -- a +8.4 s shift in p50 as concurrency grew.
  System scaling broke down progressively: low-coverage devices (4, 8, 14, 16, 18, 19, 20)
  lost more than half of their expected windows; device 20 produced none.  The phase scheduler
  itself slipped by 12 minutes cumulatively by the last device.  Together these
  place the practical saturation point of this Flink-path architecture, on this hardware,
  below 20 concurrent producers.

